WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:03.770
This series takes a closer look at each part of HASTE.

00:04.180 --> 00:07.720
In this episode: the building embeddings workflow, where you

00:07.740 --> 00:09.380
click buildings and get instant

00:09.400 --> 00:11.470
predictions, right in the browser.

00:15.300 --> 00:17.980
We start in the Spokane project, and add a new

00:18.000 --> 00:20.910
image layer, with the building labeling workflow.

00:25.273 --> 00:27.183
Here, we're using Planet data:

00:27.553 --> 00:30.523
SkySat post-event imagery from August 4th.

00:30.787 --> 00:32.607
Then, we add pre-event Bing Maps

00:32.627 --> 00:34.837
aerial imagery, and building footprints.

00:44.733 --> 00:48.313
Next, we click Embed, and choose DINOv2, a

00:48.333 --> 00:50.943
vision foundation model, in its small size.

00:52.703 --> 00:55.603
SkySat pixels are half a meter wide, so we upscale

00:55.623 --> 00:58.773
each crop two times to give the model more detail.

00:59.513 --> 01:02.073
For every footprint, HASTE averages DINOv2's

01:02.093 --> 01:04.273
features inside the building's outline.

01:04.293 --> 01:09.043
That gives 384 numbers that capture how each building looks.

01:09.933 --> 01:13.573
About a minute and a half later, all 2,061 buildings

01:13.593 --> 01:17.383
are embedded. Now we open the interactive labeler.

01:22.133 --> 01:24.903
A swipe compares pre- and post-event imagery.

01:25.373 --> 01:28.083
Burned homes are left as pale, ash-gray pads.

01:30.467 --> 01:32.237
Labeling is just clicking.

01:32.527 --> 01:33.977
Four burned homes first.

01:34.287 --> 01:37.097
Then we switch to Intact and click four that survived.

01:39.000 --> 01:40.570
With only eight labels,

01:40.660 --> 01:43.070
a classifier trains right in the browser,

01:43.200 --> 01:44.470
in a fraction of a second,

01:44.520 --> 01:46.510
and predicts every building in view.

01:47.567 --> 01:49.217
Zoom out, and it's a start,

01:49.387 --> 01:51.997
but it flags far too many buildings as damaged.

01:54.067 --> 01:55.577
So, where is it unsure?

01:55.947 --> 01:57.667
The uncertainty view shows confident

01:57.687 --> 02:00.677
buildings in blue and uncertain ones in red.

02:02.167 --> 02:05.537
We label the most uncertain buildings, ten at a time.

02:05.887 --> 02:09.237
Each click retrains the model and the map updates instantly.

02:11.733 --> 02:13.943
Round after round, the uncertainty fades.

02:19.763 --> 02:22.853
88 labels in all, each one placed where it helps most.

02:29.400 --> 02:32.470
Zoomed out, the uncertainty has almost vanished,

02:35.600 --> 02:38.010
and the predictions now trace the burned neighborhoods.

02:39.200 --> 02:42.020
We save the labels, and one more click predicts

02:42.040 --> 02:45.710
all 2,061 buildings and saves the results.

02:50.033 --> 02:52.753
The results viewer colors every footprint by its

02:52.773 --> 02:56.403
prediction, red for damaged and green for intact.

03:01.923 --> 03:04.373
Swipe to the pre-event imagery to check its work.

03:10.133 --> 03:12.133
Download the scored buildings as a

03:12.153 --> 03:14.823
GeoPackage or open the assessment report.

03:15.393 --> 03:17.943
Later in the series, we'll dig into these outputs.

03:19.533 --> 03:22.903
HASTE is open source under the MIT license.

03:23.373 --> 03:26.483
Deploy it to Azure with a single azd up command,

03:26.573 --> 03:28.783
or run it locally with Docker Compose.

03:29.233 --> 03:32.163
Find it on GitHub at microsoft/haste.
